<item>
  <title>What sparse autoencoders don’t tell you about a circuit</title>
  <link>https://setareh.example.com/writing/2026-08-11-sae-limits/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<p>A sparse autoencoder gives you a dictionary of directions in activation space and a claim that those directions are more interpretable than neurons. That claim is doing a lot of work, and this note is about where it stops holding.</p>
<section id="the-setup" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-setup">The setup</h2>
<p>Given activations <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x%20%5Cin%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%5Ed">, an SAE learns an encoder and decoder such that</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Chat%7Bx%7D%20=%20W_%7B%5Ctext%7Bdec%7D%7D%20f(x)%20+%20b,%20%5Cqquad%20f(x)%20=%20%5Ctext%7BReLU%7D(W_%7B%5Ctext%7Benc%7D%7D(x%20-%20b)),%0A"></p>
<p>trained to minimise reconstruction error under an <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cell_1"> penalty on <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f(x)">:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cmathcal%7BL%7D%20=%20%5Cunderbrace%7B%5ClVert%20x%20-%20%5Chat%7Bx%7D%20%5CrVert_2%5E2%7D_%7B%5Ctext%7Breconstruction%7D%7D%20+%20%5Clambda%20%5Cunderbrace%7B%5ClVert%20f(x)%20%5CrVert_1%7D_%7B%5Ctext%7Bsparsity%7D%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>The interpretability claim is that the columns of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?W_%7B%5Ctext%7Bdec%7D%7D"> correspond to human-legible features <span class="citation" data-cites="cunningham2023sparse">(Cunningham et al., 2023)</span>. The mechanistic claim — which is different, and stronger — is that those features are the units the model itself computes over <span class="citation" data-cites="olah2020zoom">(Olah et al., 2020)</span>.</p>
</section>
<section id="where-the-two-claims-come-apart" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="where-the-two-claims-come-apart">Where the two claims come apart</h2>
<div class="callout callout-style-default callout-note callout-titled">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-title-container flex-fill">
<span class="screen-reader-only">Note</span>The short version
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>A dictionary element can be legible to you and still not be a variable the model uses. Legibility is a property of the projection; causal relevance has to be tested separately.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Three things to check before treating a feature as mechanistic:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>Ablation actually changes behaviour.</strong> If zeroing the feature leaves the downstream logits untouched, you have found a correlate, not a cause.</li>
<li><strong>The effect survives a better baseline.</strong> Mean ablation and zero ablation disagree, sometimes badly — I wrote about this <a href="../../insights/2026-08-02-one-gpu-setup/">separately</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The feature is stable across seeds.</strong> Dictionaries trained on the same activations with different seeds should recover overlapping features. Often they don’t.</li>
</ol>
</section>
